# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <ctype.h>

int isalnum(int c);
int isalpha(int c);
int isblank(int c);
int iscntrl(int c);
int isdigit(int c);
int isgraph(int c);
int islower(int c);
int isprint(int c);
int ispunct(int c);
int isspace(int c);
int isupper(int c);
int isxdigit(int c);
int tolower(int c);
int toupper(int c);
```

# DESCRIPTION

These functions classify a character <c>, given as the value of an `unsigned char` or as `EOF`, and map case.
The only locale is "C", and only the 128 ASCII values belong to any class.
Each `is` function returns 1 if <c> is in its class and 0 if not.

| Function | Class |
| `isalnum` | letters and digits |
| `isalpha` | `A` to `Z` and `a` to `z` |
| `isblank` | space and tab |
| `iscntrl` | 0 to 31 and 127 |
| `isdigit` | `0` to `9` |
| `isgraph` | 33 to 126: printing characters but the space |
| `islower` | `a` to `z` |
| `isprint` | 32 to 126 |
| `ispunct` | graphic characters that are not letters or digits |
| `isspace` | space, and 9 to 13 (tab, newline, vertical tab, form feed, carriage return) |
| `isupper` | `A` to `Z` |
| `isxdigit` | `0` to `9`, `A` to `F` and `a` to `f` |

`tolower` returns the lower case letter for an upper case <c>, and `toupper` the upper case letter for a lower case one.
Any other <c> is returned unchanged.

These are functions, not macros, and use no table.
A value that is neither an `unsigned char` nor `EOF` is undefined in C; here it is in no class and maps to itself.
Values from 128 to 255 are in no class.

